[center][color=#00aa90]mirrors[/color]  ·  pull or push on cron  ·  keep forks warm[/center]

[description]

When Actions-style workflows are enabled, pipelines are YAML-driven similar in spirit to other platforms: jobs, steps, caches, and secrets scoped to the repo. [b]Runners[/b] must exist with matching labels; self-hosted runners are common on small installs to avoid trusting shared compute.

[b]Mirrors vs Actions:[/b] mirroring solves “keep another copy of Git history fresh”; Actions solves “run commands when events happen.” You might combine both: mirror for HA, Actions for tests on the primary.

[b]Webhooks:[/b] POST JSON payloads to your services on repository events—signature verification should be mandatory to prevent spoofed deliveries. Rate-limit outbound calls on tiny hardware.

[b]Matrix builds:[/b] where supported, fan out across OS or Go versions like larger CI systems; watch total wall time on constrained runners.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] treat outbound automation as part of your threat model—validate webhook sources, rotate tokens, and cap concurrent jobs so the host stays responsive for human developers.
